    {
      "id": "asm",
      "name": "Asm198x",
      "purpose": "Give retro programs a maintainable toolchain that respects existing source dialects.",
      "headline": "First-program builds agree with reference assemblers.",
      "assessment": "Released Asm198x 0.0.71 builds the selected C64, Amiga and NES first programs with independent reference-byte agreement. This is useful authoring evidence for those sources, not a blanket dialect-compatibility promise.",
      "demonstrated": "ACME, native vasm and ca65/ld65 agree with the selected first-program outputs. The browser\u2019s downloaded NES source rebuilds into identical cartridge bytes, and only the intended colour byte changes between its red and blue variants.",
      "gap": "The joined examples cover a small part of the CPU and dialect surface. Legacy reference tests can skip when external tools or sources are absent; a green general suite alone does not establish those comparisons ran.",
      "checkpoint": "Compare the next NES lesson with ca65/ld65, checking its segment placement and a deliberate invalid operand as well as successful output.",
      "checkpoint_reason": "This follows a concrete teaching program beyond the already demonstrated starter and keeps successful comparisons separate from skipped prerequisites.",
